  Scott's trick; collection principle; Hilbert's epsilon
=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=
$)

  $c Scott $.

  $( Extend class notation with the Scott's trick operation. $)
  cscott $a class Scott A $.

  ${
    $d x y A $.
    $( Define an operation for Scott's trick, expressed as in Equation 9.3 of
       [Jech] p. 72.  Scott's trick collects all sets that have a certain
       property and are of the smallest possible rank.  The resulting
       collection is guaranteed to be a set (see ~ scottex ).  Under this
       definition, the property in question is represented by membership in a
       class.  See ~ scottab for the case where a wff (meta)variable is used to
       represent the property.  (Contributed by Rohan Ridenour, 9-Aug-2023.) $)
    df-scott $a
      |- Scott A = { x e. A | A. y e. A ( rank ` x ) C_ ( rank ` y ) } $.
  $}

  ${
    $d ph x y $.  $d x y A $.  $d x y B $.
