  $c CHOICE $.

  $( Wff for an abbreviation of the axiom of choice. $)
  wac $a wff CHOICE $.

  ${
    $d f x $.
    $( The expression ` CHOICE ` will be used as a readable shorthand for any
       form of the axiom of choice; all concrete forms are long, cryptic, have
       dummy variables, or all three, making it useful to have a short name.
       Similar to the Axiom of Choice (first form) of [Enderton] p. 49.

       There is a slight problem with taking the exact form of ~ ax-ac as our
       definition, because the equivalence to more standard forms ( ~ dfac2 )
       requires the Axiom of Regularity, which we often try to avoid.  Thus, we
       take the first of the "textbook forms" as the definition and derive the
       form of ~ ax-ac itself as ~ dfac0 .  (Contributed by Mario Carneiro,
       22-Feb-2015.) $)
    df-ac $a |- ( CHOICE <-> A. x E. f ( f C_ x /\ f Fn dom x ) ) $.
  $}

